  [38;2;153;153;153mSearched for [1m1[0m[38;2;153;153;153m pattern, called hivemind (ctrl+o to expand)[39m

[38;2;255;255;255m●[39m There's one recorded decision on this in hivemind: [1m"Pantry [0m
  [1mstorage: SQLite, single file next to the app"[0m
  ([38;2;177;185;249mdecision-456d94e3…[39m).

  [1mWhy SQLite:[0m
  - The old JSON-file store lost writes when two requests
    arrived at the same time. SQLite's transactional writes fix
    that.
  - It's still a single file next to the app ([38;2;177;185;249mpantry.db[39m), so
    there's no server to run. [38;2;177;185;249mpantry/store.py:14[39m just calls
    [38;2;177;185;249msqlite3.connect(PATH,[39m [38;2;177;185;249m…)[39m.

  [1mOptions[0m [1mit[0m [1mruled[0m [1mout:[0m
  1. [1mKeep[0m [1mthe[0m [1mJSON[0m [1mfile.[0m Rejected because of the lost writes.
  2. [1mPostgres.[0m Called "overkill for a one-person app" because
     it's a separate server you'd have to run and back up.

  [1mCaveats:[0m
  - The decision's status is still [1mproposed[0m, not accepted. A
    Claude agent session recorded it, and no human ever reviewed
    or accepted it. The code went ahead anyway in commit
    [38;2;177;185;249m8443c86[39m.
  - It has no evidence attached, such as a benchmark or a
    reproduction of the lost writes. The rationale is the only
    record.

  If this reasoning still holds, you could mark the decision
  accepted so the record matches what shipped. If you're
  reconsidering Postgres, for example because more than one
  person will use the app, the "one-person app" reasoning is
  what would need to change.

